is the chief exec  andrew haynes say hello mr haines thanks  for joining us on the program this  morning another day another strike what  you're going to do about it  well we have put a very good deal on the  table eight percent over two years no  compulsory redundancy guarantee  additional benefits to staff and their  family members  and we think the right thing to do now  is to put that to a vote of my staff  and that's the way that this will get  called off because when people see that  deal  i believe that they will recognize it's  a good deal and it's also a fair deal  because it doesn't ask taxpayers to pay  more and it doesn't ask for fair payers  passengers to pay more either  but it is a real cut  isn't it  well it's not everything everybody would  want of course it's not but what we've  tried to do is be creative we've looked  at the range of things that the rmt have  said they're concerned about so it is  eight percent over two years and it's  got that critical no compulsory  redundancy guarantee so it gives a  commitment of jobs to people and i think  job security matters a lot and it gives  discounted travel which you know many  colleagues of mine in network have said  they've wanted for 20 30 years so when  you're looking in the round i think it's  a really good deal for them  uh you see it's tricky isn't it i mean  you you earn an absolute fortune don't  you compare to a lot of people that work  for you and they'll be standing on that  picket line saying it's all right for  him fat cat  yeah well look this isn't about my pay  that was it this is about the fact that  uh we have a costly living crisis in  this country but we also have a shift in  how people are using the railway there's  a big two billion pound hole because  passengers are working work from home  much more they're doing less business  travel and we can either bridge that by  being more efficient or we can say  taxpayer pay more or we can say whack up  fares i think we should do the self-help  and actually create a more efficient  railway and then we can give job  security then we can give better pay  rises in due course and we can have a  thriving railway for the economy of  britain  yeah but you say it's not about your  payroll your pay but you know 600 000  pounds as opposed to somebody getting 25  000 pounds you can see why they might be  a little bit upset about that  i i can  but it's not about my pay but look i've  led from the front i've taken a  significant reduction compared to any of  my predecessors and during the pandemic  i  took a voluntary temporary pay cut so  like we shouldn't get distracted by that  because if i was paid nothing it  wouldn't change this deal  but i can absolutely demonstrate i'm  leading from the front in taking and  taking that pain i'm not asking any of  my colleagues to do that what i'm saying  is let's come up with a fair deal that  actually works for people the people of  britain as well as my colleagues in  network royale  do you think that trade let's put it to  the vote i mean why not put in the vote  sure do you think that trade unions have  too much power  i think it's really wrong that when we  come up with a package like that we  listen to them we spend months and  months talking to them and we try to  deal with all the different issues that  the people within network rail  then don't get a chance to vote and  that's what i think is fundamentally  wrong when we put a fair deal on the  table surely in a democratic country  people should get the chance to choose  whether or not to accept it or not  so you do think that trade unions have  too much power  well i  yes in the extent i do in the in the  extent to which that uh they are able to  reject those sort of deals without even  consulting  my staff that feels to me to be wrong  um yesterday we had um the chief  secretary to the treasury simon clark on  the program we were talking about this  and he told me that strikes are a police  politicized attempt to make capital do  you agree  so i didn't quite catch the the uh the  quote sorry could you say it again  absolutely um it's noisy where you are  this morning it was the chief secretary  to the treasury and that's um simon  clark and he said on the programme  yesterday that strikes are a politicized  attempt to make capital and i'm asking  whether you agree with him sir  look look i think uh  there are look there is a reality of a  cost of living crisis in this country so  i don't think i'm not saying that there  isn't a real need to come up with a fair  pay deal what i would say is the goal  posts keep moving and every time we get  to the point of thinking we've reached a  settlement or reaching a milestone  the rmt walk away that is a fact twice  now we've come very close  to making real progress and then they  walk away at the last minute i'm not  going to judge their motives but i can  talk from my experience we've met a lot  of their needs and yet we don't seem to  be making progress you have to ask them  what motivates them okay  i did actually um he was on the program  a little bit earlier on mick lynch um  what have you actually offered them  so we've offered eight percent over two  years we've offered no compulsory  redundancy guarantee we've offered  deeply discounted travel for colleagues  and their  and their families we've offered cash  bonuses  uh you know it's a very comprehensive  package of course it's not everything  that people would want but i think when  you look at it in the round it really  meets the fundamental needs and also by  the way it guarantees no changes to  terms and conditions the rmt have been  saying we're looking to rip up people's  terms and conditions  that is a lie we are not making any  changes to terms and conditions we've  said we will not ask people to do  anything they're not already contracted  to do  do you think if you weren't negotiating  with mick lynch you might have got it  over the line by night  no i doc i don't think this is about the  personalities i think it's about i think  it's about the wider dynamics within the  rmt

